  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\", fails to distinguish two replies that the rule requires it to tell apart. Reply A is exactly: '```json\\n{\"a\":1} {\"b\":2}\\n```\\n{\"c\":3}' (where \\n denotes a newline). Reply B is exactly: '{\"c\":3}'. For Reply A, the rule requires the answer to be the object {\"b\":2}, because it is the last object inside the code fence, and a fence holds one. For Reply B, the rule requires the answer to be {\"c\":3}, because there is no fence and it is the last top-level object. The function, however, gives the same answer for both: it returns {\"c\":3} for Reply A because the fenced content '{\"a\":1} {\"b\":2}' is not a single JSON value and is passed over, so the function falls back to the top-level pool and picks the last top-level object, which is {\"c\":3}. This observation would show the conjecture false: if the function returned {\"b\":2} for Reply A and {\"c\":3} for Reply B, or if the documented rule were interpreted to mean that a fence containing multiple objects is not 'a fence holds one' and thus the top-level fallback is correct."
